{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/trailofbits/skills/pulls/227","id":4179743671,"node_id":"PR_kwDOQ55KR875IdO3","html_url":"https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/pull/227","diff_url":"https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/pull/227.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/trailofbits/skills/pull/227.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/trailofbits/skills/issues/227","commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/trailofbits/skills/pulls/227/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/trailofbits/skills/pulls/227/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/trailofbits/skills/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/trailofbits/skills/issues/227/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/trailofbits/skills/statuses/d0da397a7861a4c25cbf13d7511fc5d444cd1905","number":227,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"Rebuild supply-chain-risk-auditor around a deterministic collector","user":{"login":"e-q","id":6894112,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY4OTQxMTI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6894112?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q","html_url":"https://github.com/e-q","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Replaces the `gh`-only audit method in `supply-chain-risk-auditor` with a deterministic collector and renderer bundled with the skill, and rewrites the skill around them. The old method could not deliver its own criteria: repository contributors are not registry publish rights, and `gh` sees no download counts or ecosystem-keyed advisories. Version 1.0.1 → 2.0.0 (method replacement, per the #217 precedent).\n\n## What it does now\n\nTwo stdlib-only Python scripts do the measuring; the model adds judgment on top (remediation, replacement candidates, narrative), labeled as judgment and written in report register.\n\n- `collect.py` queries OSV, the npm and PyPI registries, the Go module proxy, deps.dev, OpenSSF Scorecard, and GitHub, and emits a JSON artifact.\n- `render.py` turns the artifact into a Markdown report of facts only.\n\nFor npm, PyPI, and Go it measures: version-matched advisories for direct dependencies and for the full lockfile-resolved transitive tree (advisories only at that depth); abandoned or archived upstreams; deprecated/yanked releases; npm publisher concentration; install-time scripts; and the two Scorecard checks that name a concrete mechanism (Dangerous-Workflow, Binary-Artifacts). Download volume, publish provenance, and security policy are reported as context, never flagged.\n\nThe structure enforces the honesty rules rather than documenting them:\n\n- Every criterion resolves to assessed-clean, assessed-flagged, or unassessable-with-a-reason; a coverage table bounds every claim.\n- An empty advisory answer counts as clean only for a package proven to exist: a registry document (npm, PyPI), a module-proxy answer (Go), or a registry integrity hash/source for lockfile entries. Private-registry, git, and vendored entries are named as unverifiable with reasons, never counted clean.\n- Coverage must reconcile; a run that measures nothing exits non-zero; the renderer refuses an artifact whose flags and coverage disagree; third-party text is escaped before it reaches the report.\n\n## Measured basis\n\n- **Task evals** (`evals/`, three committed fixtures with graded expectations, run with independent grader agents against a no-skill baseline): the skill passed ~92% of skill-agnostic assertions vs ~60% for the baseline, at roughly half the wall clock. One run per configuration; run-to-run variance is unmeasured. The baseline did not fabricate figures — its failures were internal inconsistency (counts contradicting its own artifacts), unlabeled advice mixed into measurement, and overclaimed coverage. The skill's measured edge is reproducibility (the report regenerates byte-for-byte from the artifact) and self-consistency, not raw accuracy.\n- **Triggering**: with the plugin loaded, three canonical audit phrasings triggered the skill as the first tool call (3/3, one run each); precision on ten near-miss negative queries was 100% across a five-iteration description-optimization loop. Recall on oblique phrasings is unmeasured — the synthetic harness put it at 8–17%, and how much of that is harness artifact is undetermined. The shipped description was the loop's best of five candidates.\n- **Adversarial review**: a briefed reviewer attacked the code, docs, and eval record (20 findings). Its two high-severity findings — Go module existence asserted but never measured, and a transitive sweep with no existence gate — are fixed in this diff with mutation-checked tests. Its benchmark-honesty corrections are folded into the numbers above.\n- Verified live against axios, express, fastapi, jupyterhub, alertmanager, and a private Python project; findings spot-checked by hand against OSV.\n\n## Known limitations, chosen deliberately\n\n- Install/build breakage is out of scope: the audit works from manifests and lockfiles alone and never installs, builds, or executes anything.\n- PyPI publisher ACLs and download counts, Go registry ACLs, and PyPI install-time execution (wheel vs. sdist) are structurally unassessable and reported as such.\n- OSV `querybatch` pagination is not followed, so advisory counts are floors for packages with enormous advisory sets.\n- Ecosystems beyond npm/PyPI/Go are refused, not improvised. The trigger eval set deliberately labels a Rust query should-trigger so the skill fires and refuses; anyone re-running the description-optimization loop should reject candidate descriptions that claim ecosystems the collector does not parse (four of five generated candidates did).\n\n## Testing\n\n`make check` (85 offline tests through the collector's own cache format; suite is mutation-checked), both loadability checks, and the full pre-commit hook set pass locally.\n\n## For reviewers\n\nThe judgment calls that most need eyes, each with its measured rationale in code comments: staleness flags at two years; single-human npm publishers flag; downloads/provenance/security-policy demoted to informational; only two Scorecard checks flag; full criteria for direct deps but advisories-only for the transitive tree. The strongest check is running it on a repo you know and asking whether the report would survive being pasted into a deliverable.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n","labels":[],"milestone":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"created_at":"2026-07-31T17:38:59Z","updated_at":"2026-08-10T20:27:17Z","closed_at":"2026-08-10T20:27:15Z","merged_at":"2026-08-10T20:27:15Z","merge_commit_sha":"e6066e7db1fd57cb35f9a534781ceec595327feb","assignee":{"login":"e-q","id":6894112,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY4OTQxMTI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6894112?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q","html_url":"https://github.com/e-q","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/e-q/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com